WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.433
Stripe takes card payments, adds Apple Pay and Google Pay, remembers cards, and

00:00:06.433 --> 00:00:08.862
tells your store about disputes.

00:00:08.862 --> 00:00:13.783
This video shows how to enter your keys, what Connect does, what buyers

00:00:13.783 --> 00:00:16.020
see, and how disputes appear.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.885
Most shoppers expect to pay by card, and many prefer Apple Pay or

00:00:24.885 --> 00:00:28.970
Google Pay, where a fingerprint replaces typing a card number.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.922
A store also has to follow what happens after:

00:00:32.922 --> 00:00:38.340
refunds, failed payments, and the rare dispute, where a bank takes the money back.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:44.670
The Stripe connection takes card payments, adds the wallets, remembers cards for signed

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.850
in buyers, and listens for what Stripe reports.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.057
This demo never contacts Stripe.

00:00:51.057 --> 00:00:55.130
It shows what you do, and what your buyers and staff see.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:03.212
You enter your Stripe keys, test keys first, so you can try a

00:01:03.212 --> 00:01:04.950
sale without real money.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.556
Connect creates a webhook, so Stripe can tell your site about payments, refunds

00:01:10.556 --> 00:01:14.840
and disputes, and registers your domain for Apple Pay and Google Pay.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:19.017
At checkout, buyers pay with a wallet or a card.

00:01:19.017 --> 00:01:22.380
Signed in buyers can keep a card for next time.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.930
When Stripe reports a dispute or a risky payment, the order shows it.

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:34.749
Open Settings, Payments, Stripe.

00:01:34.749 --> 00:01:41.071
Make an account at stripe.com, and copy your keys from the Developers, API keys page.

00:01:41.071 --> 00:01:45.169
Use the Test mode switch to choose test or live keys.

00:01:45.621 --> 00:01:50.425
Enter the Test Publishable Key, which the card form on the payment step

00:01:50.425 --> 00:01:52.671
uses, and the Test Secret Key.

00:01:52.671 --> 00:01:53.731
Click Save.

00:01:54.391 --> 00:01:58.374
The Status shows the Connect button once a secret key is saved.

00:01:58.374 --> 00:02:03.685
Production fields appear only when you tick Enable Production, and the webhook signing

00:02:03.685 --> 00:02:06.051
secret is optional, under Advanced.

00:02:10.251 --> 00:02:14.234
Connect checks your keys, and creates a webhook at Stripe:

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:20.831
an address on your site that Stripe calls to report payments, refunds, failed payments and disputes.

00:02:21.581 --> 00:02:25.627
It registers your site's domain for Apple Pay and Google Pay.

00:02:25.627 --> 00:02:30.338
A site that is only reachable on your own computer cannot be registered,

00:02:30.338 --> 00:02:32.451
but card payments still work.

00:02:33.201 --> 00:02:38.025
Disconnect removes the webhook, and clears the keys this page saved, so Stripe

00:02:38.025 --> 00:02:40.723
payments stop until you enter them again.

00:02:40.723 --> 00:02:43.881
Keys set in the application settings stay in use.

00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:48.749
A site that is not public can forward Stripe's events with the Stripe

00:02:48.749 --> 00:02:53.031
command line tool, and paste the signing secret into the optional field.

00:02:57.242 --> 00:03:00.627
Apple Pay and Google Pay need nothing to turn on.

00:03:00.627 --> 00:03:05.798
After a successful Connect on a public domain, the wallet buttons appear above

00:03:05.798 --> 00:03:10.662
the card form, when the buyer's device has a wallet and an amount is due.

00:03:11.113 --> 00:03:15.047
A signed in buyer paying for a one time purchase can tick Save

00:03:15.047 --> 00:03:16.782
this card for next time.

00:03:16.782 --> 00:03:19.733
The card is kept by Stripe, not by your site.

00:03:20.184 --> 00:03:25.482
Next time the payment step offers Pay with, their saved cards and A new card.

00:03:25.482 --> 00:03:30.844
Under My account, Payment methods lists their cards, and lets them remove one.

00:03:35.044 --> 00:03:39.505
When a bank opens a dispute, Stripe tells your site, and the order

00:03:39.505 --> 00:03:43.904
shows a Disputed badge, so nobody ships or refunds it by mistake.

00:03:44.654 --> 00:03:48.281
A payment Stripe thinks may be fraud is shown as Risky, so you

00:03:48.281 --> 00:03:49.934
can look before you ship.

00:03:50.684 --> 00:03:56.994
With Workflows on, Stripe's events start workflows, for example to tell your team about a dispute.

00:03:57.744 --> 00:04:02.284
Answer a dispute in your Stripe dashboard, where you can send the evidence.

00:04:06.484 --> 00:04:08.014
Go live in steps:

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:12.604
test keys and a test order first, live keys when you are ready.

00:04:13.354 --> 00:04:14.687
A mobile shopper:

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.714
a wallet payment in a second.

00:04:17.464 --> 00:04:19.408
A returning customer:

00:04:19.408 --> 00:04:21.784
a saved card and one click.

00:04:22.534 --> 00:04:23.724
A chargeback:

00:04:23.724 --> 00:04:27.044
the order says Disputed, and your team is told.

00:04:29.044 --> 00:04:34.519
Enter your test keys, connect, and let buyers pay with a wallet or a saved card.

00:04:34.519 --> 00:04:36.174
Thanks for watching.

